<p id="p2">Following <a href="https://www.macstories.net/ios/apple-frames-4-1-now-with-support-for-iphone-duo-iphone-18-pro-and-macbook-pro-with-proled-xdr-display/" rel="noopener noreferrer">this week’s update to my Apple Frames shortcut</a> and <a href="https://github.com/viticci/frames-cli" rel="noopener noreferrer">Frames CLI</a> with support for the iPhone Duo and 18 Pro, I’ve prepared another small update for more device bezels Apple released a couple of days ago: the Apple Watch Ultra 4 and the new iPad mini (which <a href="https://www.macstories.net/reviews/ipad-mini-2026-review-pushing-the-boundaries-with-apples-smallest-ipad/" rel="noopener noreferrer">John reviewed earlier this week on MacStories</a>).</p>
<p id="p3">If you’re using the Frames CLI, you can update to version 1.7.0 <a href="https://github.com/viticci/frames-cli" rel="noopener noreferrer">from the GitHub repo</a> or ask your agent to do it for you. If you’re a Shortcuts user, you can get the latest version of Apple Frames (4.2.0) below or from the <a href="https://www.macstories.net/shortcuts/" rel="noopener noreferrer">MacStories Shortcuts Archive</a>. In either case, you’ll have to download an updated asset pack with the new bezels, which will automatically replace the older one.</p>
<p id="p4">As a general recommendation for Shortcuts users, before trying to run the shortcut from the Home Screen or Siri, you’ll want to run it manually at least once so you can grant the initial set of permissions.</p>
